Title: Rethinking cardiovascular risk stratification in TKI-treated chronic myeloid leukemia.
Abstract: Tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs) targeting BCR::ABL1 have transformed chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) into a chronic condition with near-normal life expectancy, but peripheral artery disease (PAD) has emerged as a major source of morbidity, particularly with nilotinib and ponatinib. Current cardiovascular management relies on general population risk scores and ankle-brachial index (ABI) measurement, tools not designed to capture TKI-specific vascular biology or detect subclinical arterial damage.

